{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01BfvbYL9CztZgoY3uMozS7d", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "  This agent's report was delivered to you as a message from \"a40b8d4fca726128d\" (its SubagentHandback call). Read it there; it is not repeated here.\n  \nagentId: a40b8d4fca726128d (use SendMessage with to: 'a40b8d4fca726128d', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 21114\ntool_uses: 1\nduration_ms: 4541</usage>"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "96dd0fb2-494f-43fc-a94b-64b154cbaa30", "uuid": "2a4a97e4-9fbf-4c1d-9ce3-dc472538c2ed", "timestamp": "2026-10-02T07:14:06.981Z", "tool_use_result": {"status": "completed", "prompt": "Write a 120 word explanation of what a hash map is. Do not make any tool calls; answer directly from your own knowledge. Return only the explanation text.", "agentId": "a40b8d4fca726128d", "agentType": "general-purpose", "handback": "send", "handbackReport": {"text": "A hash map is a data structure that stores key-value pairs and provides very fast lookup, insertion, and deletion, typically in constant time on average. It works by passing each key through a hash function, which converts the key into an integer. That integer is then mapped to an index in an underlying array, often called a bucket array, where the value is stored. Because different keys can produce the same index, a situation called a collision, hash maps use strategies such as chaining, where each bucket holds a small list, or open addressing, where another free slot is probed. When the array becomes too full, the map resizes and rehashes its entries to keep operations fast. Keys must be hashable and comparable for equality."}, "harnessNoteCount": 1, "harnessTailCount": 0, "harnessSectionHash": "63b4b80b74d8c06e", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This agent's report was delivered to you as a message from \"a40b8d4fca726128d\" (its SubagentHandback call).